---
swarmId: "{{.projectId}}-swarm"
displayName: "{{.displayName}} swarm"

roles:
  - name: "lead"
    description: "Routes ad-hoc (non-review) tasks and recovery checkpoints."
    model: "{{.llmModel}}"
    runtime:
      image: "localhost/vornik-agent:latest"
      envVars:
        VORNIK_MAX_TOOL_ITERATIONS: "30"
    permissions:
      allowedTools:
        - "current_time"
        - "file_read"
        - "read_many_files"
        - "grep"
        - "glob"
        - "mcp__vornik__grant_step_tools"
        - "memory_search"

  - name: "reviewer"
    description: "Reviews pull-request diffs via the github-review workflow."
    model: "{{.llmModel}}"
    runtime:
      image: "localhost/vornik-agent:latest"
      envVars:
        VORNIK_MAX_TOOL_ITERATIONS: "60"
    permissions:
      # No git_status/git_diff/git_log/git_show here: the
      # github-review workflow's fetch_diff step (forge.fetch_diff)
      # fetches the complete unified diff daemon-side and the
      # review prompt explicitly tells the agent NOT to run git —
      # granting git tools to this role would violate least
      # privilege for a capability it's told never to use.
      allowedTools:
        - "current_time"
        - "file_read"
        - "read_many_files"
        - "grep"
        - "glob"
        - "memory_search"
---

# {{.displayName}} swarm

A review-focused swarm for **{{.repo}}**: the reviewer runs the
`github-review` workflow's diff reviews; the lead routes anything
else. Edit roles + prompts at
`/ui/swarms/{{.projectId}}-swarm/edit`.

## Role prompts

### lead

You route ad-hoc tasks for a code-review project covering
**{{.repo}}**. Most work arrives as PR-review tasks through the
github-review workflow; route anything else through adaptive
judgment, preferring the most direct path.

### reviewer

You review pull requests for **{{.repo}}**. For each diff: check
correctness first (logic errors, unhandled failure paths,
concurrency hazards), then security (injection, authz gaps,
secret handling), then maintainability. Report only findings that
matter, each with file:line, a one-sentence problem statement,
and a concrete failure scenario. No style nitpicks a linter would
catch. End with a short verdict paragraph.
